description:
Edit repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/feature-compose.ts. One change, one file.

Inside the exported function inertRegexEditRefusal there is a block that occurs exactly once in the file:

    if (!differs) {
      return null;
    }

Match it exactly as written, including the four-space indentation on the `if` line and six spaces on the `return`. Replace ONLY the `return null;` inside that block with a returned refusal string, leaving the `if (!differs) {` line and its closing brace untouched. The replacement returns a template string reporting that the patch is an inert regex literal edit, naming the old and new sources and the probe-string count, and telling the author to change the behaviour or make no edit.

The identifiers p.oSrc, p.nSrc and cands.size are already in scope at that point. Do not rename them, do not add imports, and do not touch the other `return null;` statements elsewhere in the same function, which are correct early guards for a missing diff, an unbalanced hunk, an unparseable literal and an empty pair list.

Why this is required. This function is a refusal gate: returning a string refuses the patch, returning null allows it. The !differs branch is the one case the gate exists for - an edit whose only change is a regex literal behaving identically on every probe string - and it currently returns null, allowing exactly what it was built to refuse. The gate is a no-op in its own purpose branch while still appearing present in the code.

Evidence. The running substrate gate self-probe reports this rule as the single failing rule of five, refused_hostile false, and the recovery ladder moved to broken on that basis. Against the corrected function the probes own hostile fixture, a regex gaining a duplicate alternative, is refused, while a genuine widening that changes what matches is still allowed, so restoring the refusal does not make the gate over-fire.

Change nothing else, and edit no other file.

